---
/**
 * Provident fund for Tax Year 2026-27: the mid-year ceiling change, the pension split and the
 * ceiling-or-full-wages choice. Every figure is computed at build time by the engine.
 */
import DocLayout from '../layouts/DocLayout.astro';
import { bracketPath } from '../lib/brackets';
import { pfGuideData } from '../lib/explainers';
import { formatRupees } from '../lib/format';
import { DEDUCTION_CAPS, EMPLOYER_CONTRIBUTION_PERQUISITE_LIMIT } from '../lib/tax/deductions';

const data = pfGuideData();
const top = data.wageRows.at(-1)!;
const twenty = data.modeRows.find((r) => r.lpa === 20)!;
// From the rounded figures in the table, so the text matches them exactly.
const twentyCost = Math.round(twenty.takeHomeAtCeiling) - Math.round(twenty.takeHomeOnFull);
const lakh = (ctc: number) => `₹${(ctc / 1_00_000).toFixed(2).replace(/\.?0+$/, '')} lakh`;
const ceiling = formatRupees(data.ceiling);
const previous = formatRupees(data.previousCeiling);
---

<DocLayout
  title="Provident fund: the new ₹25,000 ceiling, month by month"
  description="The EPF wage ceiling rose from ₹15,000 to ₹25,000 on 17 September 2026. What comes out of your salary each month this year, how your employer's share splits into pension, and what PF on full wages costs."
  updated="2026-10-09"
  ogImage="provident-fund"
  about={['Employees Provident Fund', 'EPF wage ceiling', 'Employees Pension Scheme']}
  deva="बचत"
>
  <p>
    You and your employer each pay 12% of your wages into the Employees' Provident Fund. Most
    employers pay only up to the statutory wage ceiling, and on 17 September 2026 that ceiling rose
    from {previous} to {ceiling} a month. If your wages are above {previous}, your PF deduction goes
    up from October, and so does your employer's contribution.
  </p>
  <p>
    Many salary calculators still use {previous} for the whole year. This one applies the change
    month by month.
  </p>

  <h2>The ceiling changed in September</h2>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Wage months</th>
          <th scope="col">Wage ceiling</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>April to August 2026</td>
          <td>{previous}</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>September 2026</td>
          <td>
            {previous} for 16 days, {ceiling} for 14 days
            <span class="block text-xs text-ink-3">
              {formatRupees(data.septemberCeiling)} for the month
            </span>
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>October 2026 to March 2027</td>
          <td>{ceiling}</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>From April 2027</td>
          <td>{ceiling}</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    S.O. 2702(E) of 29 May 2026 had re-notified the ceiling at {previous} under the Code on Social
    Security. S.O. 5109(E) of 17 September 2026 replaced it with {ceiling} from the date of
    publication, for the provident fund, the pension scheme and EDLI insurance alike.
  </p>

  <h2>What comes out of your salary</h2>
  <p>
    Your own 12%, when your employer contributes up to the ceiling. "Wages" here are Labour Code
    wages: Basic and DA, plus anything the <a href="/labour-code-wages">50% rule</a> adds back.
  </p>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Wages a month</th>
          <th scope="col">April to August</th>
          <th scope="col">September</th>
          <th scope="col">October to March</th>
          <th scope="col">This year</th>
          <th scope="col">From 2027-28, a year</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {data.wageRows.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td>
              {formatRupees(r.wages)}
              {r === top && ' or more'}
            </td>
            <td>{formatRupees(r.beforeChange)}</td>
            <td>{formatRupees(r.september)}</td>
            <td>{formatRupees(r.afterChange)}</td>
            <td>{formatRupees(r.thisYear)}</td>
            <td>{formatRupees(r.nextYear)}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    At {previous} or less nothing changes. Between the two ceilings the deduction rises to 12% of
    your wages. At {ceiling} or more it goes from {formatRupees(top.beforeChange)} to{' '}
    {formatRupees(top.afterChange)} a month, and next year's total is{' '}
    {formatRupees(top.nextYear - top.thisYear)} higher than this year's.
  </p>
  <p>
    Your employer pays the same amount again. If your offer letter counts employer PF inside your
    CTC, as most do, that extra contribution can also come out of your CTC, usually by trimming
    special allowance. The drop in in-hand from October is then up to twice the rise in your own
    deduction, before tax.
  </p>

  <h2>Your employer's 12% and the pension split</h2>
  <p>
    Part of the employer's contribution goes to the Employees' Pension Scheme: 8.33% of wages up to
    the ceiling. The rest goes into your PF account.
  </p>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Wages of {ceiling} or more</th>
          <th scope="col">Employer's 12%</th>
          <th scope="col">To the pension scheme</th>
          <th scope="col">To your PF account</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Ceiling of {previous}</td>
          <td>{formatRupees(top.beforeChange)}</td>
          <td>{formatRupees(data.epsBefore, true)}</td>
          <td>{formatRupees(top.beforeChange - data.epsBefore, true)}</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Ceiling of {ceiling}</td>
          <td>{formatRupees(top.afterChange)}</td>
          <td>{formatRupees(data.epsAfter, true)}</td>
          <td>{formatRupees(top.afterChange - data.epsAfter, true)}</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The pension share does not sit in your PF balance and does not earn PF interest; it counts
    toward a pension under the scheme instead. With the higher ceiling both parts grow, so more goes
    to the pension scheme and more to your PF account.
  </p>

  <h2>PF on the ceiling or on full wages</h2>
  <p>
    Some employers contribute 12% of your full wages instead of stopping at the ceiling. That is
    allowed when you and your employer agree to it, and it puts much more into PF, but it costs
    monthly in-hand. Here is the difference using the calculator's standard structure, with Basic at
    half of CTC and employer PF inside it.
  </p>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">CTC</th>
          <th scope="col">In hand, PF at the ceiling</th>
          <th scope="col">In hand, PF on full wages</th>
          <th scope="col">Into PF a year, at the ceiling</th>
          <th scope="col">On full wages</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {data.modeRows.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td>
              <a href={bracketPath(r.lpa)}>{r.lpa} LPA</a>
            </td>
            <td>{formatRupees(r.takeHomeAtCeiling)}</td>
            <td>{formatRupees(r.takeHomeOnFull)}</td>
            <td>{formatRupees(r.pfAtCeiling)}</td>
            <td>{formatRupees(r.pfOnFull)}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    In-hand figures are a month, on the cheaper regime and averaged across this year's ceiling
    change; PF is your share and your employer's together. At {twenty.lpa} LPA, PF on full wages
    puts {formatRupees(twenty.pfOnFull - twenty.pfAtCeiling)} more a year into PF and costs{' '}
    {formatRupees(twentyCost)} a month in hand. That money is still yours, as long-term savings
    rather than salary.
  </p>

  <h2>Tax on PF</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Your contribution</strong> counts toward the {formatRupees(DEDUCTION_CAPS.sec80C)}{' '}
      limit under section 123 (the old 80C), but only in the old regime. The new regime gives no
      deduction for it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your employer's contribution</strong> is not taxed, unless employer contributions to
      PF, NPS and superannuation together exceed{' '}
      {formatRupees(EMPLOYER_CONTRIBUTION_PERQUISITE_LIMIT)} a year. The excess is taxed as part of
      your salary; the calculator includes it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Interest</strong> on your own contributions above{' '}
      {formatRupees(data.interestThreshold)} in a year is taxable every year, or above ₹5,00,000 if
      your employer does not contribute. With PF on full wages and Basic at half of CTC, your own
      share passes {formatRupees(data.interestThreshold)} above a CTC of about{' '}
      {lakh(data.interestAbove)}. The calculator does not include this interest, because it depends
      on your PF balance and the rate declared each year.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Try it with your own figures</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="/">calculator</a> uses the ceiling by default. To see PF on full wages, open{' '}
    <a href="/?m=advanced&pf=actual">advanced mode with full-wage PF</a>, and compare the two.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sources: Employees' Provident Funds Scheme, 2026 (G.S.R. 525(E), 29 June 2026) under the Code on
    Social Security, 2020; S.O. 2702(E) of 29 May 2026 and S.O. 5109(E) of 17 September 2026 on the
    wage ceiling. <a href="/how-it-works">Full method</a>.
  </p>
</DocLayout>
